          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+integration"} motivic integration
            %em Greenberg scheme
            refers to one of multiple generalizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arc+spaces"} arc spaces
            (here referred to as “arc schemes”). Greenberg schemes are built on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            over a
            %em complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+valuation+ring"} discrete valuation ring
            (DVR). In the locally Noetherian setting, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a complete DVR then Greenberg schemes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+schemes"} formal schemes
            generalize this prior notion of Greenberg schemes. Greenberg schemes associated to such formal schemes are the setting for motivic integration.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a complete DVR, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{m}
            be its unique
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideal"} maximal ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi 𝔪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k=R/\mathfrak{m}
            be its residue field.
          %h3#the_ring_schemes_
            The ring schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_n
          %p
            For every non-negative integer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Alg
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_n\colon\mathbf{Alg}_k\to\mathbf{Ring}
            from the category of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔪
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A\mapsto A\otimes_k R_n = A\otimes_k R/\mathfrak{m}^{n+1}
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is equicharacteristic (that is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            have the same characteristic). Denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_\infty
            the limit of these functors, which is again representable. As schemes, these are identified with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}_k^{n+1}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mi k
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}_k^\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_\infty
            are not the same as the usual ones on affine space.
          %p
            In the mixed characteristic case, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(-)
            be the functor which assigns a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+Witt+vectors"} ring of Witt vectors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathscr{R}_n\colon A\mapsto W(A)\otimes_{W(k)} R_n
          %p
            is not always representable, but its fpqc-sheafification (its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafification
            over the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fpqc+site"} fpqc site
            %em is
            representable; in this case, we replace the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_n
            with its fpqc-sheafification.
          %h3#functorial_description_of_
            Functorial description of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_n
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒪
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\mathscr{O}_Y)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+space"} locally ringed space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒪
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n(Y)=(Y,\mathscr{R}_n(\mathscr{O}_Y))
            whose structure sheaf is valued in
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Hom
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℛ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U\mapsto\mathrm{Hom}_k(U,\mathscr{R}_n)
          %p
            which has the structure of an
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℛ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{R}_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n(-)
            is isomorphic to the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\times_{k} R_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n(Y)
            is an
            = succeed "-scheme." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_n
          %p
            With this construction, the
            %em Greenberg functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_n
            can be defined abstractly as the right adjoint to the functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Hom
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Sch
                        %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{Gr}_n(X)(-)\coloneqq
                  \\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Sch}_R}(h_n(-), X).
          %p
            This functor is representable; write the corresponding scheme as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_n(X)
          %p
            The Greenberg schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_n(X)
            form an inverse system, where the morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_m(X)\to\mathrm{Gr}_n(X)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\ge n
            come from the obvious natural transformations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⇒
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n\Rightarrow h_m
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Hom
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Sch
                        %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{Gr}_\infty(X)(-)\coloneqq
                  \\lim_n\,\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Sch}_R}(h_n(-),X)
          %p
            is also represented by a
            = succeed "-scheme." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %h3#greenberg_schemes_of_formal_schemes Greenberg schemes of formal schemes
          %p
            To any locally Noetherian
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔛
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{X}
            %em Greenberg scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔛
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gr}_n(\mathfrak{X})
            by simply applying the already constructed Greenberg functor to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔛
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝔛
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi R
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{X}_n=\mathfrak{X}\times_{R} R_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔛
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔛
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{Gr}_\infty(\mathfrak{X})\coloneqq
                  \\lim_n\,\mathrm{Gr}_n(\mathfrak{X})
          %p
            again is representable, hence defines a
            = succeed "-scheme," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em Greenberg scheme
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔛
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{X}
          %p
            This construction generalizes Greenberg schemes associated to locally Noetherian
            = succeed "-schemes," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
